PyThreatAnalyzer third-party data notices

TweetFeed SHA-256 IOCs
Source: https://github.com/0xDanielLopez/TweetFeed
License: CC0-1.0 (as declared by upstream).
Suggested attribution: “IOC data from TweetFeed (CC0)”. Imported snapshots are
recent community-reported indicators, not a complete historical corpus. The
snapshot URL, retrieval time, payload checksum and count are recorded in
tweetfeed_meta.json when populated.

PRX-SD Built-in YARA rules
Source: https://github.com/openprx/prx-sd-signatures
The upstream source table declares the Built-in sub-source MIT. The aggregate
repository itself is Apache-2.0; its LICENSE and README are retained with the
import. Only the Built-in YARA sub-source is selected. Full upstream commit and
rule checksums are recorded in yara_rules/META.json. Upstream rule metadata uses
the author name "prx-sd" but does not provide a separate copyright year or
per-rule license file. The bundled notice records the MIT terms and identifies
the upstream attribution as "prx-sd"; it is not a verbatim upstream copyright
notice. Recheck the license interpretation before each update or broader
redistribution.

ReversingLabs YARA rules
Official source: https://github.com/reversinglabs/reversinglabs-yara-rules
License: MIT, as declared by the official upstream repository. Retain the
upstream MIT copyright and permission notice with imported rules, together
with full commit and rule checksums in yara_rules/META.json.

NIST NSRL RDS
Source: https://www.nist.gov/itl/csd/secure-systems-and-applications/national-software-reference-library-nsrl/nsrl-download-0
NIST states the RDS is public domain and redistribution is free and encouraged;
retain NIST attribution and identify modifications. The SQLite database is a
local post-installation resource and is not redistributed in this project.
Verify downloaded archives against NIST's published checksum. The local setup
helper records the checksum of the extracted SQLite database in ignored local
metadata.

All imported data files are listed in checksums.txt. Hash matches and YARA
matches are analysis signals; they do not replace analyst review.

Legacy MalwareBazaar feed
The project no longer includes a MalwareBazaar snapshot in its default
distribution or active hash database. The legacy importer remains available
for maintainers, requires an ABUSE_CH_KEY, and must not be used to redistribute
data until the current upstream terms and authorization have been reviewed.
